0\n    KTPH001\n      \n        Translation\n      \n    KTPH002\n  KTPH003\n  \n","f":["<a href=\"#translation\" class=\"md-nav__link\">","<span class=\"md-ellipsis\">","</span>","</a>"]},{"s":35822,"e":35969,"k":"block","t":"\n  KTPH000\n    KTPH001\n      \n        Enterprise policy\n      \n    KTPH002\n  KTPH003\n  \n","f":["<a href=\"#enterprise-policy\" class=\"md-nav__link\">","<span class=\"md-ellipsis\">","</span>","</a>"]},{"s":36350,"e":36440,"k":"block","t":"Release notesKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#release-notes\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":36449,"e":36546,"k":"block","t":"What changed in the current release. The version this guide was built for is shown in the footer.","f":[]},{"s":36566,"e":36642,"k":"block","t":"v2.13.0KTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#v2130\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":36687,"e":36809,"k":"block","t":"Connection loss and reconnectKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#connection-loss-and-reconnect\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":36824,"e":37326,"k":"block","t":"KTPH002A lost connection no longer closes the tabKTPH003 — when an established SSH connection drops (network outage, VPN cut, server gone), the tab now stays open in a red KTPH000 state with a red status bar showing when the connection was lost, instead of silently closing as if you had typed KTPH001. Reconnect in place with a double-click on the bar or tab, or via KTPH004ReconnectKTPH005 in the context menus; a normal remote logout still closes the tab as before.","f":["<code>(DISCONNECT)</code>","<code>exit</code>","<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":37336,"e":37749,"k":"block","t":"KTPH001Connection loss is detected within about ten secondsKTPH002 — korTTY actively probes the server over the SSH connection (the same technique as OpenSSH's KTPH000) and treats two consecutive unanswered probes as a lost connection, instead of waiting minutes for a TCP timeout. The terminal cursor also stops blinking in a disconnected tab, so a dead session no longer looks alive.","f":["<code>ServerAliveInterval</code>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":37759,"e":38399,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000Automatic reconnectKTPH001 — a new KTPH002Settings → Terminal → Automatically reconnect lost connectionsKTPH003 option (on by default) reconnects a lost tab on its own, with delays growing from 3 seconds up to one attempt per minute and a countdown in the red status bar. Permanent failures such as a wrong password, a changed host key, or a configuration refusal are never retried automatically, and while a session journal is deciding how to continue, the journal's own reconnect choice takes precedence. See KTPH004Terminal sessions → Connection lossKTPH005.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>","<a href=\"../features/terminal.html#connection-loss-and-automatic-reconnect\">","</a>"]},{"s":38428,"e":38506,"k":"block","t":"PrivacyKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#privacy\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":38521,"e":39055,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000Anonymous usage statistics are now pre-selected during first-run setupKTPH001 — the checkbox next to the master-password setup (KTPH002Anonymous data for application optimizationKTPH003) starts ticked, so sharing is the default and clearing it before you confirm is the opt-out. An organization policy that forbids telemetry locks the checkbox to the enforced state instead. What is collected is unchanged: voluntary, anonymous, EU servers, and changeable any time in KTPH004Settings → PrivacyKTPH005.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<a href=\"anonymous-data.html\">","</a>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":39087,"e":39171,"k":"block","t":"AppearanceKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#appearance\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":39186,"e":39530,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000A first installation now starts with KTPH001Match display resolutionKTPH002 onKTPH003 — korTTY scales its UI font size to the screen from the very first launch instead of only after someone finds the setting. Updating an existing installation never changes it: whatever it had stays.","f":["<strong>","<a href=\"../reference/settings/appearance.html#ui-font-size\">","</a>","</strong>"]},{"s":39563,"e":39649,"k":"block","t":"TranslationKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#translation\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":39664,"e":40193,"k":"block","t":"KTPH001Yandex Translate works againKTPH002 — it still spoke the retired Translate API v1.5, whose credentials Yandex stopped accepting, so the provider was dead for anyone who selected it. It now calls Cloud Translate v2 with the current KTPH000/IAM-token authentication, batches requests to the API's limits, and no longer sends a leftover v1.5 API URL to a host that is gone. A rejected key's fragments, which Yandex echoes back inside its own error message, are now redacted before anything reaches a log file.","f":["<code>Api-Key</code>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":40203,"e":40506,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000Microsoft Translator now reaches regional and custom-domain resourcesKTPH001 — a new optional KTPH002Azure regionKTPH003 setting, shown only while Microsoft Translator is selected, is required by any Azure resource that is not global; such a resource previously could not be reached at all.","f":["<strong>","</strong>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":40516,"e":40750,"k":"block","t":"KTPH000DeepL now recovers from a wrong Free/Pro host guessKTPH001 — the key suffix korTTY uses to pick a host is only reliable for older Free keys; a 403 response now retries once against the other host instead of failing outright.","f":["<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":40789,"e":40887,"k":"block","t":"Enterprise policyKTPH000","f":["<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#enterprise-policy\" title=\"Permanent link\">&para;</a>"]},{"s":40902,"e":41674,"k":"block","t":"KTPH003Administrators can forbid AI web accessKTPH004 — a new KTPH005KTPH000KTPH006 key switches off every internet-access mode an AI profile can select (Tavily, Bright Data, Brave, SearXNG, LM Studio Toolpack). It is enforced at three levels rather than one: stored profile modes are reset whenever settings are loaded or saved, the dropdown is locked in the AI Manager and in KTPH007Settings → AIKTPH008, and korTTY refuses to build a service for a profile whose mode is still enabled — so neither a hand-edited KTPH001 nor the window between two clamps re-opens web access. Cloud profiles still reach their own provider; deny the KTPH002 feature for that.","f":["<code>[rule.ai-profiles] allow-internet</code>","<code>global-settings.xml</code>","<code>ai</code>","<strong>","</strong>","<a href=\"../reference/enterprise-policy.html\">","</a>","<strong>","</strong>"]},{"s":41744,"e":41760,"k":"block","t":"Earlier releases","f":[]},{"s":41768,"e":42143,"k":"block","t":"Only the current release is listed here, so the guide stays short in every language it is translated into. Every version is on the KTPH002GitHub releases pageKTPH003; the curated notes for earlier versions are kept in the repository, in KTPH000 and KTPH001.","f":["<code>app-docs/release-notes-archive.md</code>","<code>app-docs/RELEASE_NOTES.adoc</code>","<a href=\"https://github.com/chardonnay/korTTY/releases\">","</a>"]},{"s":43021,"e":43036,"k":"text","t":"\n  Back to top\n","f":[]},{"s":43279,"e":43320,"k":"text","t":"MIT-licensed · Developed by Daniel Mengel","f":[]},{"s":43366,"e":43378,"k":"text","t":"\n    korTTY ","f":[]},{"s":43386,"e":43393,"k":"text","t":"v2.13.0","f":[]}]}